<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rules version="4">
    <!-- Load this base, then rules_<language>.xml and rules_<language>_<COUNTRY>.xml (SearchLocales: the locale of
         the map data, "en_US" for a US map, "de_CH" for name:de of a Swiss map). This base holds only rules that are
         true in every language: today there are none, words and their abbreviations belong to a language file. A map
         without a locale ("" - World basemap, an unknown map) gets no word rules at all.

         A stored name keeps the words of OSM: no rule rewrites it.
           <index>  the OBF writer adds an alternative name to the names of objects, once, with the locale of the map
                    and of the name tag (name:de on an Italian map is de_IT). Only what a query word cannot produce:
                    a phrase, a glued or a tokenizer-lost form. The rule is a regexp with one form, the attribute to:
                      <rule object="street" mode="All" from="(?iu)\b([\p{L}\p{M}]{2,})straße\b" to="$1str"/>
                    object (on <rule>): the owner of the name - street, locality, boundary, postcode, poi, "a,b" for
                    several, "*" (the default) for any. mode: All replaces all matches, the default requires one.
           <query>  the search gives a query word its forms, with the locale of every OBF it reads. The rule of a plain
                    word with one meaning has the attributes to and object:
                      <rule from="pl" to="Place" object="street"/>
                    a word with several meanings lists them as <to> elements, the first is the main one:
                      <rule from="st"><to object="street">Street</to><to>Saint</to></rule>
                    A form is one word; object is the owner of the names it applies to ("*" by default). Every
                    form gets a reverse form of the same owner ("street" -> "st", "saint" -> "st"); a reverse form is
                    one step and never takes the forms of the word it leads to, so "saint" does not become "street".
                    A one-letter word needs an object for its forms. object="building" without to marks the word as a
                    part of a house number; to="" makes the word ignorable (a conjunction, an article).
           <common> words that do not count as unmatched words of a name, separated by spaces.
         A pair belongs to one side: a word of an index form is no word of <query>.

         The key of a query rule is its word (from, ignoring case), of an index rule object and from. A rule of a lower
         layer with the key of an upper rule replaces it whole; enabled="false" removes it; two rules with one key in
         one file are an error. <common enabled="false"> removes words of the upper layers. -->
</rules>
